From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:08:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] target/arm, arm_gic: resume an aarch64 KVM snapshot under TCG
 and a TCG snapshot under KVM

- cpu_post_load(): translate the incoming cpreg list when it comes from the
  other accelerator (source = KVM iff it has KVM_REG_ARM_FW* entries):
  drop registers the destination doesn't list and ID registers; move the
  virtual timer between KVM's swapped uapi ids (TIMER_CNT is CNTV_CVAL's
  encoding, TIMER_CVAL is CNTVCT's) and TCG's CNTV_CVAL_EL0; tolerate
  read-only mismatches; keep the destination's reset value for registers
  the stream lacks (quietly; trace event arm_cpu_handoff).
- new subsection cpu/virt-count (TCG): the guest virtual count at save, so
  a KVM destination sets KVM_REG_ARM_TIMER_CNT and a TCG destination
  without EL2 rebuilds CNTVOFF.
- TCG destination: re-arm the EL1 timers after a load, and drive their
  output lines again when the VM starts: the GIC section loads after the
  CPU and a KVM GIC's state carries no timer line level, so a timer that
  had fired before the source stopped never interrupted again.
- cpu/wfxt-timer: a KVM destination loads the TCG-only timer into a dummy
  instead of asserting in VMSTATE_TIMER_PTR.
- fail |= (not =) for registers only in the stream.
- arm_gic: KVM's vGICv2 ABI exports GICC_PMR as VMPriMask (>>3) and the
  APRs as GICH_APR, which kvm-arm-gic stores as-is; mark such streams
  (subsection arm_gic/kvm-cpuif) and convert on a cross-accelerator load.
  Without it a KVM PMR 0xf0 reads 0x1e under TCG and masks every IRQ.

Needs kvm-steal-time=off on the KVM side (pvtime RAM block) and the KVM
host's CNTFRQ on the TCG cpu (cntfrq=). ARM_HANDOFF_DEBUG=1 dumps the
translation and the GIC CPU-interface state.

